return 0; but drand_48 always returns -1. What is wrong with it, any ideas?

answers.body:
Calling omp_get_num_threads() outside of a parallel region always returns 1 since only one thread (the master thread) executes code outside of those regions. You should use omp_get_max_threads() instead. Also it is a Good Idea TM to initialise the __x member. The equivalent of srand48(seed) would be: .__x[0] = 0x330E;
.__x[1] = (unsigned short)(seed & 0xffff);
.__x[2] = (unsigned short)(seed >> 16); Apart from that, drand48_r() is the reentrant version of drand48() . Note that reentrancy is not the same as thread-safety , with the latter being what you probably need. In GLIBC (e.g. Linux) erand48() keeps track of the last value in a global variable and is therefore not thread-safe (nor is it reentrant), but on OS X it does not modify global variables (it also does not keep track of the last output) and is therefore reentrant (when both calls are passed disjoint xsubi arrays). It is also safe to make multiple calls to erand48() from different threads as long as no other thread calls srand48() or lcong48() . The reason is that all PRNGs share the multiplicative and additive constants used in the congruent formula. If you need to support different constants in each thread, you should probably directly copy the implementation of drand48_r() from GLIBC.

