Your question is essentially a duplicated of another one I answered recently . Even after you get your code to compile you will find race conditions and false sharing. Instead of giving the same answer let me add something new. In your code you try and define the chunks for the for loop manual and additionally you also save the partial sum per thread and then merge the result after the parallel section. There is no good reason to do either of these things for what you're doing. But let me give two examples where you need to do this. The only reason I can think of to define the chunks manually is when doing induction to reduce the number of computations per iteration . In your case you can change your function to use induction like this: double pi = 0.0;
for(double x=0.5*step; x<max; x+=step) {
    pi += 1.0/(1.0+x*x);
}
pi *= 4*step To do this with OpenMP requires that you define start and end range of the for loop: double pi = 0.0;
#pragma omp parallel reduction(+:pi)
{
    int ithread = omp_get_thread_num();
    int nthreads = omp_get_num_threads();
    int start = ithread*n/nthreads;
    int finish = (ithread+1)*n/nthreads;
    double startx = (start+0.5)*step;
    double finishx = (finish+0.5)*step;
    for(double x=startx; x<finishx; x+=step) {
        pi += 1.0/(1.0+x*x);        
    }       
}
pi *= 4*step; As I already stated, induction is the only case I can think of where you need to do this. Your code also saves the result of the reduction per thread. The only reason I can think of doing this is if the operation you are doing is not commutative . Matrix multiplication is not commutative as A*B is not equal to B*A in general. But your operation is commutative but let assume it's not.  In that case you could do the reduction like this double pi = 0.0;
int nthreads;
double *ppi;
#pragma omp parallel
{
    double pi_private = 0.0;
    int ithread = omp_get_thread_num();
    #pragma single
    {
        nthreads = omp_get_num_threads();
        ppi = new double[nthreads];
    } 
    #pragma omp for
    for(int i=0; i<n; i++) {
        double x = (i+0.5)*step;
        pi_private += 1.0/(1.0+x*x);
    }
    ppi[ithread] = pi_private;
}
for(int i=0; i<nthreads; i++) {
    pi += ppi[i];
}
pi *= 4*step;
delete[] ppi; Incidentally, in order to do a reduction the operation needs to be associative. But floating point arithmetic is not strictly associative. When doing reductions with floating point arithmetic one has to accept that the result may not be reproducible. In any case the simple thing to do in your case is: double pi = 0.0;
#pragma omp parallel for reduction(+:pi)
for(int i=0; i<n; i++) {
    double x = (i+0.5)*step;
