answers.body:
The for loop is executed the same number of times as if there was no OpenMP. Every time it is executed, the message is printed. It doesn't matter which thread prints it and how many threads there are. The for loop OpenMP worksharing  just means that the work of printing the message n times is divided between the threads, but the number of passes remains. Note you have a race condition with the ID variable. You should make it private or declare it inside the loop. The value of the variable can have meaningless values when printed. Declaring it inside the loop: #pragma omp for schedule(static,2)
for(i=0;i<16;i++)
{
   int ID=omp_get_thread_num();
   printf("Hello(%d)\n",ID);
   printf("World(%d)\n",ID);
} or int ID=omp_get_thread_num();
#pragma omp for schedule(static,2) private(ID)
for(i=0;i<16;i++)
{
   printf("Hello(%d)\n",ID);
   printf("World(%d)\n",ID);
}

