I guess the race condition arises from the second loop nest, because different combinations of i and j have the same sum.  The best way to work around the problem would probably be to eliminate the data race.  There are a few variations on how you might do that, but the ones that come first to mind involve making the outer loop of the second nest run over the indices of pr , and the inner one over either the indices of p1 or those of p2 .  In that way, each element of pr will be modified by exactly one thread. Because that modification leads to different iterations of the outer loop requiring different amounts of work, you could consider switching to dynamic thread scheduling.  Don't assume that you need dynamic scheduling, however -- the regular way in which the amount of work varies might well make static scheduling balance out over the course of the whole computation. For example, #pragma omp parallel for private(i,j,p1,p2,m,n)
for (k = 0; k <= m + n; k++) {
    int mini = (k <= n) ? 0 : k - n;
    int maxi = (k <= m) ? k : m;

    for ( i = mini; i <= maxi ; i ++) {
        j = k - i;

        pr[ k ].re += p1[ i ].re* p2[ j ].re;
        // ...

Many functions and data openCV use the same memory addresses for different variables, for example if you have a matrix Mat A and you do Mat B = A, data matrix B are stored in the same pociciones memory A, now when you use OpenMP must make sure that when you write to a memory location, just do it from a single thread, otherwise you will get an error at runtime. Now when you use a single thread there is no problem, since it is only one thread which writes or reads a pocicion memory. On the other hand when you use functions to print screen as printf () or std :: cout, there is the possibility that the threads are delayed, that is, that while a thread prints, another thread writes to the memory locations, by thus the possibility of an error at runtime decline, but that does not mean that in the future do not exist. The solution when you use OpenMP in a loop to protect write in the same memory locations from different threads is:` #pragma omp critical
